  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Delivery of Emergency Drought Impacts Recovery Response for the Somali Urban Resilience Project Phase Two (SURP-II) in Mogadishu, Garowe and Baidoa Municipalities</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Somalia has been subject to continuous fragility since it emerged from a civil war in the 1990s. However, in 2021, Somalia&apos;s electoral and political crisis deepened leading to a violent confrontation between pro-government and pro-opposition forces. Furthermore, Al-Shabaab, which formed as a violent extremist group in the early 2000s, has continued to conduct attacks in Somalia, and to control large parts of central and southern Somalia. Attacks often target the city of Mogadishu, making any government-led project activity a potential target. Decades of conflict and fragility coupled with frequently recurring natural disasters have led to significant internal displacement of populations over the years and have had devastating impacts on the country&apos;s infrastructure and service delivery sectors. This significantly impacts the government&apos;s ability to respond to newly emerging crisis. Through this project, IOM supports a government-led emergency response to the intensifying drought crisis by providing critical basic services, including water, sanitation and hygiene, shelter, and health, to drought-induced, protracted internally displaced person and vulnerable host communities.</narrative>
  </description>
